decisions:
  - id: chrono-background-mount
    decision: Keep stopwatch/timer/pomodoro views mounted (hidden) instead of conditionally rendered
    rationale: A running stopwatch or timer must keep ticking while the user is on another tab; unmounting would reset it. They also use performance.now() so they stay accurate across throttling.
  - id: alarm-queue
    decision: Queue concurrent alarms instead of dropping or overwriting
    rationale: A second alarm firing while one is ringing was silently lost and still ran side effects (notify + one-time disable). A queue rings it after the current one, never interrupts an in-progress puzzle (key={alarm.id} + no remount), and moves side effects to a single activate() path.
  - id: standalone-app-over-dev
    decision: Build/install a real a-clock.app with a login item rather than relying on the dev server
    rationale: The user's alarms didn't fire because only the ephemeral dev server was running and it was killed. A real installed app that launches at login and lives in the tray is required to trust it as an alarm clock.
  - id: pmset-wake
    decision: Wake the Mac from sleep with pmset via an osascript admin prompt, opt-in per the wake toggle
    rationale: A JS timer can't wake a sleeping Mac. pmset schedule wake can, but needs root, so we authorize via a one-time admin prompt and dedupe so it only re-prompts when the earliest alarm changes.
learnings:
  - Dev and release Tauri builds share a bundle id but use different webview origins (localhost vs tauri://), so localStorage does not carry over — alarms set in dev do not appear in the installed app.
  - pmset schedule wake requires root; invoking it from a GUI app means an osascript 'with administrator privileges' prompt, so scheduling must be deduped to avoid repeated prompts.
  - Giving the ringing overlay key={alarm.id} makes each alarm start fresh while the same active alarm never remounts, which preserves an in-progress puzzle across the 4x/sec time re-render.
